Looking at the diff: the first alternative of `YAML_FLOAT_EXPLICIT_PATTERN` in `float_json.ts` changed from `[-+]?[0-9]+...` (optional sign) to `[-+][0-9]+...` (mandatory sign).

This pattern is used **only** when `resolveYamlFloat$1` is called with `isExplicit === true`, i.e. when a scalar carries an explicit tag resolved by `floatJsonTag`. `floatJsonTag` appears only in `JSON_SCHEMA` (the default `load` uses `CORE_SCHEMA`/`floatCoreTag`, and `dump` uses `DUMP_SCHEMA`, so neither path is affected — which explains why the existing suite stays green).

With the patch, an unsigned value no longer matches any alternative (alternatives 2–4 all require a leading `.`), so under `JSON_SCHEMA`:

- `!!float 5`, `!!float 0.5`, `!!float 1e3` → `NOT_RESOLVED` → `constructScalar` throws `cannot resolve a node with !<tag:yaml.org,2002:float> explicit tag`, whereas before the change they resolved to `5`, `0.5`, `1000`.
- Signed forms (`!!float -5`) still work, so signed-only tests pass.

The YAML 1.2 JSON schema (and JSON itself) has no sign requirement on numbers, and the implicit (plain-scalar) path in the same resolver still accepts unsigned values — so this is an unintended behavioural break, not an equivalent refactor.

